WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:04.200
Walking out on Amazon
because they lying to us,

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.060 position:59%
not caring about our health or safety.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.930 position:66%
We want better equipment, protective gear.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.310
We have no masks.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:11.750
We want hazard pay.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.610
If I lose my job, I lose my job.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:16.386
I shouldn't be speaking
like this, but I don't care.

00:00:16.386 --> 00:00:18.070 position:56%
I don't care, I don't give a anymore

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.900
cause I'm scared too.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.110 line:0%
In the middle of this coronavirus crisis,

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.110 line:0%
I am obviously one of the lucky ones.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.190 align:start position:0% line:0%
I can just get my work
done at home on a laptop

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.553 size:88% position:44% line:0%
with my tiny coworker, Liam,
here, who's four months old.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.960
But obviously there are many,
many people in this country

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.650
who aren't in that position

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.120
who still have to go to
jobs at grocery stores,

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.730
warehouses, factories, etc.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:43.490
It's a little hard to tell
exactly how many people

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.810 position:61%
are still going to those kinds of jobs,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.840 position:61%
but the Brookings Institution estimates

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.470
that before the COVID crisis,

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:53.320
there were as many as 62
million people in those jobs,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.080
and no doubt many of them
are still going to work.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.520 position:64%
And what started to happen in recent days

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.310
is since those jobs have
become a lot more risky,

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.470
some of those workers
are starting to strike

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.990
for better working conditions.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.870 align:start position:0% line:0%
In the past couple weeks,
we've seen walk-outs

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:13.470 line:0%
at Purdue, at McDonald's, at InstaCart,

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.220
at Whole Foods, at Amazon.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.300 position:64%
There is this national and global crisis,

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.310
which is being experienced
in a particularly acute way

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.180
in a number of work places
that many people in America

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.440
have not thought of those
places where workers

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:28.650
have much leverage.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.570
And some of the calculation
that workers make

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.900
about whether it's worth
confronting the boss,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.060 position:61%
whether it's worth acting collectively,

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.300
that calculation is shifted
when people have real fears

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.190 position:66%
about it being dangerous to come into work

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.973
if they don't have changes made.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.250 position:61%
Workers at an Amazon warehouse facility

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.920
on Staten Island are
staging a walkout today.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.240
They're complaining about
the fact that their facility

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.830
has not been cleaned
out since a worker there

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:02.900
tested positive.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.010
They think up to six other workers

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.540
may have tested positive as well,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.870
and they want Amazon to close
down that distribution center

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:13.350
for a period of time, have
it entirely sanitized.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.070
Tell me a little bit more
about the Amazon situation.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.170
We've heard about a lot about
this Staten Island warehouse,

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.040
but I guess there's a couple
other locations as well.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.878
There've now been walkouts
at Amazon warehouses

00:02:23.878 --> 00:02:28.790
in Staten Island, in
Chicago, and in Michigan.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:31.540
In each of these cases,
you have workers saying

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.620
the company is not doing
enough to keep them safe.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.770
I'm doing this because of my health

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.270
and my fellow workers as well.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:40.380
It should be closed down.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.640
It should be cleaned properly.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.390 line:0%
The key complaint they had was around

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.600 size:81% position:41% line:0%
what the workers thought
was a lack of transparency

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.040 line:0%
around just how they were being told

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.560 align:start position:0% line:0%
about positive COVID cases
within their workforce.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.430 line:0%
They say sometimes it took too long,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.765
so they walked out over it,

00:02:54.765 --> 00:02:57.030 position:63%
and pressed management with some demands

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.320 position:63%
about sick pay should they get the virus

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.956
and that kind of thing.

00:03:00.956 --> 00:03:03.640 position:61%
If you have sick employees at your job,

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.710
and they pass the coronavirus
down to your customers,

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:07.850
what are you gonna say then?

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.920
We need that protection as well.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.930
We need to know that, we want
to feel safe while working.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:15.120
And that's it.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.680
Amazon released a statement
on the day of the walkout

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.190
saying that the protestors
accusations were unfounded,

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.650 position:64%
and saying we have taken extreme measures

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.660
to keep people safe, tripling
down on deep cleaning,

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.170
procuring safety supplies
that are available,

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:32.180
and changing processes to ensure
that those in our buildings

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.890
are keeping safe distances.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:38.030
Amazon also offers an example
of one of the challenges

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:39.870
for this worker pushback.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.780 position:63%
A leader of the strike in Staten Island,

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.350 position:58%
the same day that the strike happened

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.190 size:78% position:39% line:0%
was terminated by Amazon.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.500 size:88% position:44% line:0%
We had a successful walkout.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:50.530 align:start position:0% line:0%
We got the message out.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.080 align:start position:0% line:0%
We got people aware,
that's what we here for.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:55.350 position:56%
Amazon says they weren't retaliating

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:56.820
for raising safety concerns.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.650 position:66%
They were actually enforcing safety rules,

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:04.200
but that strike leader
says he was being punished

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.910
for standing up for his coworkers.

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:10.250
The question of what happens
to workers who stand up

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:14.460
and whether those protests
become inspirations

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.990
or cautionary tales about
how you can get yourself

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:21.160 position:59%
in an even worse situation is crucial.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.630
And people are going
to take mixed messages

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.353
from some of these examples.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:30.750
Let's talk about the
situation with Whole Foods.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.832 position:64%
Tell me about what's been going on there.

00:04:32.832 --> 00:04:36.300
To add to the strike
parade, I guess, at Amazon,

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.180
which owns Whole Foods, their
workers or a subset of them

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.100 position:58%
started holding a sick-out on Tuesday

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:44.083
where folks called in sick.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.280
They were asking for better
protections on sales floors.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.770
Some folks had wanted to
wear masks in recent weeks,

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.580
and they cited some fuzzy
guidance from management on that,

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.680 position:63%
and just again, the general feeling that

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.370 position:56%
if something were to happen to them,

00:04:58.370 --> 00:04:59.820 position:56%
they want guarantees from management

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.460 position:56%
that they'd have their costs covered

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.020 position:59%
or be able to care for family members.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.860 line:0%
Myself and all the Whole Foods employees

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:12.400 size:88% position:44% line:0%
need to keep putting as much
pressure on them as possible

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.260 align:start position:0% line:0%
to make these changes for
the employees, the customers,

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:18.770
and the public health, and the
good of the company, frankly.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:21.950
Paid sick leave,
reimbursement for testing,

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.830
hazard pay, and a firm policy
for shutting down stores

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.838
when an employee tests
positive for COVID-19.

00:05:27.838 --> 00:05:32.838
All these things have big
implications for safety,

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:37.070
not just for the employees,
but for the customers

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.290
and the public health at large.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:41.110
Have you heard about any
changes that the company

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:43.923
is going to make in
response to this action?

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:46.780
I have not, no.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:50.200
We don't know exactly how
many people called out sick,

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.870
but we talked to workers
in Illinois, New York,

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:55.910
New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Texas

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:58.093 position:61%
who said that they did that on Tuesday.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:00.670
The company says its
operations weren't affected.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:01.783
Stores remained open.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.170 position:56%
Those Whole Foods and Amazon strikes

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:08.330 position:61%
are really just the tip of the iceberg.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.150
In the past week and a half or so,

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:12.390 position:59%
we've seen demonstrations by employees

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:14.930 position:58%
at a Purdue chicken plant in Georgie,

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.460
sanitation workers in Pittsburgh,

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:20.860 position:63%
and GE factory workers in Massachusetts,

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.430
all basically advocating
for similar things,

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:26.090 position:58%
safer, more sanitary work conditions.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:28.500
Although in the case of
GE, those workers are also

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.420 position:66%
trying to get their company to switch over

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:33.800
to more manufacturing of
ventilators for hospitals.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.710
Workers at McDonald's are
also getting in on the action

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:40.210
in several states led by
the advocacy organization,

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:41.800
Fight for $15.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:46.230 align:start position:0% line:0%
We was told, we're not to
wear masks or gloves by the GM

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:47.070 align:start position:0% line:0%
here at McDonald's.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.380 align:start position:0% line:0%
She was told by McDonald's
we're not supposed

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.510 align:start position:0% line:0%
to use masks.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.570
And the reason we're out here today

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:54.220
is because we wondering
what they're gonna do

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:55.660
about our safety.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.323
Right now, they're doing nothing.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.658 position:56%
So, is any of this having an effect?

00:07:00.658 --> 00:07:02.890
Well, in some cases it
seems like the answer

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:04.500
is a cautious yes.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.280
Just in the past couple
days, we've seen McDonald's

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.070 position:63%
announce that they're going to introduce

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:11.660
better safety practices, and
that they're even considering

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.350
hazard pay for some workers.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:16.300
And Amazon has announced that
starting early next week,

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.020
it's going to be handing out
masks to all its employees

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:20.930
and doing temperature checks

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:23.450
at all of its warehouse locations.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:25.830
So it seems like some of
these workers at least

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.280
are starting to get
some of what they want.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:30.440 position:66%
The question now for many labor organizers

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:34.540
is can these short-term
concessions be parlayed

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:38.460
into more permanent
change for American labor?

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:41.580 position:59%
What's going on now creates an opening

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.150
for various kinds of organizing

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:46.430 position:56%
that might not've been there before.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.090
We will know more in the
weeks and months ahead

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:52.760
about how much these
protests change things,

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:56.160 position:61%
and to what extent they lead to changes

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:57.720
that outlast the coronavirus,

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.990
and to what extent they
can lead to organization

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:04.210
and leverage for workers
that outlast this current,

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:05.333
very unusual moment.

